  "dom_id": "step-q79199",
  "name": "Question 9",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14038344/assets/dba173a7cf462e7e67067a78c12ea05a50ea5713da0e6ddaed152055b3ce589f.png)\n\nThe function $h(x)$ is continuous and twice-differentiable for all $x.$ The graph $y={h}^{\u2033}(x)$ is shown above. At what value(s) of $x$ does $h(x)$ have a point of inflection?",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that since $h(x)$ is twice-differentiable, the tangent line to the graph exists for all real $x.$\n\nInflection points occur when a graph changes concavity. In order for the graph to change concavity, the value of the second derivative ${h}^{\u2033}(x)$ must switch signs. So, we are looking for points where the graph of ${h}^{\u2033}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis.\n\nWe can see that the graph $y={h}^{\u2033}(x)$ crosses the $x$-axis at  $x=-2,$  $x=0,$ and $x=3.$\n\nTherefore, $h(x)$ has points of inflection at $x=-2,$ $x=0,$ and $x=3.$",
